  "q": "Unified Modelling Language is normally meant for —",
  "opts": {
   "A": "Capturing the physical database design level",
   "B": "Visualizing the overall view of system and sub-systems",
   "C": "Defining the flow of data within and outside the system and sub-systems",
   "D": "Representing how users interact with the system functionalities"
  },
  "ans": "B",
  "exp": "UML's official purpose is to provide a standard, graphical way to visualize, specify, construct, and document the artifacts of a software system - giving an overall, comprehensible view of a system and its sub-systems for all stakeholders. It is not limited to physical database design (that is ER/physical schema modeling), nor solely data-flow modeling (that is DFDs), nor solely user-interaction modeling (that is use-case diagrams, just one UML diagram type among many). Because UML spans structural and behavioural views of the whole system, option (b) is the most complete and correct description.",
