  "exp": "In a datagram (connectionless, packet-switched) network, each switch/router makes an independent per-packet forwarding decision by consulting a routing (forwarding) table that maps destination address ranges to output ports; this table can change between packets of the same flow because there is no pre-established path. The destination address (a) is looked up IN that table rather than being the thing the switch \"uses\" as a mechanism, and sender address (b) and header (d) play no role in the forwarding decision itself. The wording of this question is compressed from its usual textbook form, so treat the answer as reasoned rather than certain.",
