  "exp": "In a virtual-circuit (connection-oriented) network, a global address is only used once, at connection-setup time, to establish the path; after that, every switch along the path assigns a small locally-significant Virtual Circuit Identifier (VCI), and subsequent data packets carry only this short VCI in their header instead of the full source/destination address. This is more efficient than carrying full addresses on every packet (as datagram networks do). \"Network identifier\" (c) and \"IP identifier\" (d) are not real terms used in virtual-circuit switching, and \"global address\" (a) is used only once during setup, not for ongoing data transfer.",
