  "q": "Define files. And how are they organized in a system.",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "A file is a named collection of related information recorded on secondary storage. It is the logical storage unit the user sees — the smallest allotment of named storage the operating system permits a user to create — and the OS maps it onto physical devices, so the user need not know about tracks, sectors or blocks. Typical attributes held in the directory entry are name, identifier, type, location, size, protection, and time, date and owner information. The basic operations are create, write, read, reposition (seek), delete and truncate.\n\nFiles are organised at two levels, and the question can be read either way, so both are given.\n\nOrganisation of records within a file: sequential — records stored and retrieved strictly in order, cheap and suited to batch processing; indexed sequential (ISAM) — sequentially stored records plus an index of keys, allowing direct access as well; direct or hashed — the record key is hashed to a relative block address, giving the fastest access to a single record but poor sequential retrieval.\n\nOrganisation of files within the system (directory structure): single-level; two-level, one directory per user; tree-structured, with subdirectories and absolute or relative path names; acyclic-graph, allowing shared subdirectories via links; and general-graph, which needs cycle detection and garbage collection on deletion.\n\nThe access methods built on these are sequential access, direct (relative) access, and index-based access.",
