  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "Let P(n) be the statement: Σ(i = 0 to n) i³ = [ n(n+1)/2 ]².\n\nBasis step (n = 0): LHS = 0³ = 0. RHS = [ 0·(0+1)/2 ]² = 0² = 0. Both sides equal 0, so P(0) is true. (Checking n = 1 as well: LHS = 0 + 1 = 1, RHS = [1·2/2]² = 1² = 1.)\n\nInduction hypothesis: assume P(k) holds for some arbitrary k ≥ 0, that is Σ(i = 0 to k) i³ = [ k(k+1)/2 ]².\n\nInduction step: prove P(k+1).\n\nΣ(i = 0 to k+1) i³ = Σ(i = 0 to k) i³ + (k+1)³\n= [ k(k+1)/2 ]² + (k+1)³      (by the hypothesis)\n= k²(k+1)²/4 + (k+1)³\n= (k+1)² [ k²/4 + (k+1) ]      (taking (k+1)² common)\n= (k+1)² (k² + 4k + 4)/4\n= (k+1)² (k+2)²/4\n= [ (k+1)(k+2)/2 ]²\n= [ (k+1)((k+1)+1)/2 ]²\n\nwhich is precisely P(k+1).\n\nSince P(0) is true, and P(k) implies P(k+1) for every k ≥ 0, by the principle of mathematical induction P(n) is true for all n ≥ 0.",
