  "q": "Explain the concept of Recursion.",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "Recursion is a problem-solving technique in which a function solves a problem by calling itself on a smaller instance of the same problem, until the instance is small enough to be answered directly.\n\nEvery correct recursive routine has two parts. The base case is the instance solved with no further call; it is what terminates the recursion. The recursive case reduces the problem strictly towards the base case and combines the sub-result. Omitting the base case, or writing a recursive case that does not move towards it, produces infinite recursion and a stack overflow.\n\nAt run time each call pushes an activation record (stack frame) onto the system stack holding the actual parameters, local variables and the return address; the frame is popped when the call returns. So recursion costs O(depth) extra memory even when the code is short.\n\nExample - factorial:\nfact(n) = 1 if n = 0 (base case), else n * fact(n-1) (recursive case).\nfact(4) = 4*fact(3) = 4*3*fact(2) = 4*3*2*fact(1) = 4*3*2*1*fact(0) = 4*3*2*1*1 = 24, unwound after five frames.\n\nDirect recursion is a function calling itself; indirect or mutual recursion is A calling B which calls A. Tail recursion, where the call is the last action performed, can be turned back into a loop by the compiler.",
  "points": [
   "Defines recursion as a function calling itself on a smaller instance of the same problem",
   "Names both required parts: base case (terminating condition) and recursive case that reduces towards it",
   "States that a missing or unreachable base case gives infinite recursion / stack overflow",
   "Explains the run-time cost: one activation record per call on the system stack, so O(depth) extra space",
   "Gives a worked example such as fact(4) = 4*3*2*1*1 = 24 with the unwinding shown",
   "Mentions direct vs indirect (mutual) recursion, and tail recursion being convertible to iteration"
  ],
  "exp": "",
