  "sectionB": true,
  "paper": "TECH1",
  "unit": "3",
  "sub": "Balanced expression check (stack)",
  "q": "Explain the steps to check whether a given expression is balanced or unbalanced.",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "Use a stack: the next closing bracket must match the most recently opened bracket still unclosed - exactly last-in-first-out behaviour.\n\nSteps. Create an empty stack and scan left to right, one symbol at a time.\n1. If the symbol is an opening bracket - ( , [ or { - push it on the stack.\n2. If the symbol is a closing bracket - ) , ] or } - then if the stack is empty, report unbalanced and stop (a closer with no opener). Otherwise pop the top symbol; if the popped opener is not the matching type for this closer, report unbalanced and stop.\n3. Operands and operators are ignored.\nAfter the scan, an empty stack means balanced; anything left on it means those openers were never closed, so unbalanced.\n\nTrace of { a + ( b - c ) * [ d ] } : push { ; push ( ; ) pops ( - matches ; push [ ; ] pops [ - matches ; } pops { - matches ; stack empty, so balanced.\nTrace of ( a + [ b ) ] : push ( ; push [ ; ) pops [ - wrong type, so unbalanced.\n\nFor n symbols the scan is O(n) time and O(n) worst-case stack space.",
  "points": [
   "Identifies the stack as the right structure and gives the LIFO justification (innermost open bracket closes first)",
   "Push on every opening bracket; on a closing bracket pop and compare types",
   "States the two failure conditions during the scan: pop from an empty stack, and a type mismatch on pop",
   "States the terminating condition: balanced only if the stack is empty after the full scan",
   "Traces at least one balanced and one unbalanced example, e.g. ( a + [ b ) ] fails because ) pops [",
   "Gives O(n) time and O(n) worst-case space"
  ],
  "exp": "",
  "conf": "high",
  "prov": "Jr. Grade of MES, P&E Cadre (Electrical Wing), July 2023, Paper I, Section B Q17; conventional short-answer question (5 marks) - model answer written for this app; no official MPSC answer exists for it",
