  "sub": "Deadlock avoidance (resources)",
  "q": "If an operating system possesses N resources of identical type, and these resources are shared among four processes labeled as a, b, c, and d, with respective peak demands of 3, 5, 8, and 7, then what is the value of N that would prevent the occurrence of deadlock?",
  "opts": {},
  "ans": "",
  "model": "For N units of a single resource type shared by n processes with peak (maximum) demands M1...Mn, deadlock is impossible if and only if\n\nN >= (sum of Mi) - n + 1, equivalently N >= sum of (Mi - 1) + 1.\n\nReasoning: the worst case is that every process holds one unit less than it needs and then blocks. That state consumes sum of (Mi - 1) units. If even one unit is left over, it can be given to some process, which then finishes and releases everything it holds, so the system unblocks. If no unit is left over, all processes are blocked forever.\n\nHere n = 4 and the peak demands are 3, 5, 8, 7:\nsum of Mi = 3 + 5 + 8 + 7 = 23\nN >= 23 - 4 + 1 = 20.\n\nCheck: with N = 19 the allocation (2, 4, 7, 6) uses all 19 units and every process still needs one more, so the system deadlocks. With N = 20 that same allocation leaves one spare unit, which completes some process. Hence the minimum deadlock-free value is N = 20 (any N >= 20 is safe).",
  "points": [
   "States the bound N >= sum(Mi) - n + 1 and justifies it by the worst case where each process holds Mi - 1 units",
   "Computes sum of peak demands = 3 + 5 + 8 + 7 = 23",
   "Computes N >= 23 - 4 + 1 = 20",
   "States the minimum value as N = 20",
   "Verifies with the counterexample allocation (2, 4, 7, 6) = 19 units, which deadlocks when N = 19"
  ],
