  "ans": "",
  "model": "For a single resource type R shared by n processes with maximum demands M1...Mn, the system can never deadlock if and only if the number of units satisfies\n\nN >= (sum of Mi) - n + 1.\n\nReasoning: the worst possible allocation gives every process one unit fewer than it needs, so each Pi holds Mi - 1 units and all are blocked. That state consumes sum of (Mi - 1) = sum of Mi - n units. Provide one more unit than that and at least one process can be completed; on finishing it releases all its units, which lets the next process finish, and so on. Provide exactly that many or fewer and every process can be left one unit short simultaneously, which is deadlock.\n\nHere n = 3 and M1 = 21, M2 = 31, M3 = 41:\nsum of Mi = 21 + 31 + 41 = 93\nN >= 93 - 3 + 1 = 91.\n\nCheck: with 90 units the allocation (20, 30, 40) exhausts the resource with every process still needing one more unit, so it deadlocks. Hence the minimum number of units of R required to guarantee no deadlock is 91.",
